## Why JavaScript needs asynchronous code

Imagine a task manager loading the user's tasks from a Spring Boot backend. The browser sends a request, but the server might need 50 milliseconds, 500 milliseconds, or several seconds to respond.

JavaScript should not freeze the entire page while it waits.

If JavaScript simply stopped executing until every network request finished, a slow server could make the interface feel completely frozen. You would not be able to click buttons, type into inputs, or interact with the page while waiting.

JavaScript therefore uses **asynchronous programming** for operations that finish later.

The basic idea is simple:

> Start something that may take time, continue doing other work, and handle the result when it becomes available.

This is especially important for web applications because network requests are inherently unpredictable.

## The event loop in plain words

JavaScript in the browser normally runs your code on a single main thread. You can think of this as one worker processing JavaScript instructions.

~~~widget event-loop
~~~

The browser itself can handle other work, such as timers, network activity, and user input. When that work is ready to notify your JavaScript code, the corresponding callback can be scheduled to run.

The **event loop** is the mechanism that coordinates this process.

A simplified picture looks like this:

1. JavaScript starts running your code.
2. Your code starts an asynchronous operation.
3. The browser handles the operation while JavaScript can continue.
4. When the operation is ready, its callback or Promise continuation is queued.
5. JavaScript processes that work when the current code has finished.

You do not need to memorise the internal implementation to use asynchronous JavaScript effectively. The important point is that starting an asynchronous operation does not mean the result is available immediately.

## Callbacks and setTimeout

A **callback** is a function that is provided to another function so it can be called later.

The browser's `setTimeout()` function is a simple example.

~~~js
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("One second passed");
}, 1000);

console.log("This runs first");
~~~

The callback does not execute immediately. The browser waits until the timer is ready, then schedules the callback.

The output will therefore be conceptually:

~~~text
This runs first
One second passed
~~~

The exact timing is not a guarantee that the callback runs precisely after 1000 milliseconds. The timer specifies a minimum delay before the callback can be scheduled, and JavaScript still has to be free to execute it.

### Callback-based asynchronous code

Before Promises became common, applications often represented asynchronous operations with callbacks.

A simplified example might look like this:

~~~js
function loadUser(callback) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    callback({ name: "Anna" });
  }, 500);
}

loadUser(user => {
  console.log(user.name);
});
~~~

This works, but complex applications can end up with callbacks nested inside other callbacks. This can make error handling and sequencing difficult to read.

Promises provide a more structured way to represent future results.

## Promises

A **Promise** is an object representing the eventual result of an asynchronous operation.

~~~diagram
{"type": "flow", "title": "The life of a Promise", "nodes": [{"id": "p", "label": "pending", "sub": "work in progress", "kind": "queue"}, {"id": "f", "label": "fulfilled", "sub": "has a value", "highlight": true}, {"id": "r", "label": "rejected", "sub": "has an error"}, {"id": "t", "label": ".then(value)", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "c", "label": ".catch(error)", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "fin", "label": ".finally()", "sub": "always runs", "kind": "code"}], "edges": [{"from": "p", "to": "f", "label": "resolve"}, {"from": "p", "to": "r", "label": "reject"}, {"from": "f", "to": "t"}, {"from": "r", "to": "c"}, {"from": "t", "to": "fin", "dashed": true}, {"from": "c", "to": "fin", "dashed": true}]}
~~~

A Promise has three important states:

- **pending** — the operation has not finished yet
- **fulfilled** — the operation completed successfully
- **rejected** — the operation failed

For example:

~~~js
const promise = new Promise(resolve => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    resolve("Done");
  }, 1000);
});
~~~

Initially, the Promise is pending. After the timer finishes, `resolve()` fulfills it with the value `"Done"`.

A rejected Promise represents failure:

~~~js
const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    reject(new Error("Something went wrong"));
  }, 1000);
});
~~~

You will normally consume Promises rather than create them yourself. Browser APIs such as `fetch()` return Promises.

## then, catch and finally

The `then()` method lets you handle a fulfilled Promise.

~~~js
promise.then(result => {
  console.log(result);
});
~~~

The `catch()` method handles rejection.

~~~js
promise
  .then(result => {
    console.log(result);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error(error);
  });
~~~

`finally()` runs after the Promise settles, regardless of whether it succeeded or failed.

~~~js
promise
  .then(result => {
    console.log(result);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error(error);
  })
  .finally(() => {
    console.log("Finished");
  });
~~~

This can be useful for cleanup or changing a loading state back to `false`.

## async and await

Modern JavaScript usually makes asynchronous code easier to read with `async` and `await`.

An **async function** always returns a Promise.

~~~js
async function getMessage() {
  return "Hello";
}
~~~

Even though the function returns a normal string inside its body, calling it produces a Promise.

~~~js
getMessage().then(message => {
  console.log(message);
});
~~~

`await` pauses the execution of the current async function until a Promise settles.

~~~js
async function loadData() {
  const result = await someAsyncOperation();
  console.log(result);
}
~~~

It does not freeze the browser. The surrounding async function waits for its result while the JavaScript environment can continue handling other work.

### Error handling with try and catch

With async/await, `try...catch` provides a familiar way to handle asynchronous errors.

~~~js
async function loadUser() {
  try {
    const user = await getUser();
    return user;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Could not load user", error);
    throw error;
  }
}
~~~

The `catch` block runs if the awaited Promise rejects or if code inside the `try` block throws an error.

You can also use `finally`:

~~~js
async function loadUser() {
  try {
    return await getUser();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    throw error;
  } finally {
    console.log("Request finished");
  }
}
~~~

## Fetching data with fetch

The browser provides the `fetch()` API for making HTTP requests.

Suppose your Spring Boot backend exposes:

~~~text
GET /api/users
~~~

You can request it with:

~~~js
const response = await fetch("/api/users");
~~~

`fetch()` returns a Promise that eventually produces a `Response` object.

An important detail is that `fetch()` does **not** automatically reject the Promise for HTTP errors such as 404 or 500. You should check `response.ok` yourself.

~~~js
const response = await fetch("/api/users");

if (!response.ok) {
  throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
}
~~~

`response.status` contains the HTTP status code, such as `200`, `404`, or `500`.

## Reading JSON

The response body is not automatically a JavaScript object. If the server returns JSON, call `response.json()`.

~~~js
const response = await fetch("/api/users");

if (!response.ok) {
  throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
}

const users = await response.json();
console.log(users);
~~~

`response.json()` is asynchronous and returns a Promise.

Suppose the backend returns:

~~~json
[
  { "id": 1, "name": "Anna" },
  { "id": 2, "name": "Peter" }
]
~~~

After `await response.json()`, `users` is a normal JavaScript array containing objects.

You can then transform it with the array methods from the previous lesson.

~~~js
const names = users.map(user => user.name);
~~~

## A complete GET request

A practical function might look like this:

~~~js
async function loadUsers() {
  const response = await fetch("/api/users");

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
~~~

Because `loadUsers()` is async, it returns a Promise.

A caller can use it like this:

~~~js
try {
  const users = await loadUsers();
  console.log(users);
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
}
~~~

## POST requests with JSON

GET is commonly used for retrieving data. A **POST** request is often used to send data to the backend to create something.

For example, creating a task might require sending this object:

~~~js
const task = {
  title: "Learn async JavaScript",
  completed: false
};
~~~

You can send it as JSON using `fetch()` options.

~~~js
const response = await fetch("/api/tasks", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(task)
});
~~~

The `Content-Type` header tells the server that the request body contains JSON.

`JSON.stringify()` converts the JavaScript object into JSON text suitable for the HTTP request body.

You should still check the response:

~~~js
if (!response.ok) {
  throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
}
~~~

A complete function could be:

~~~js
async function createTask(task) {
  const response = await fetch("/api/tasks", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: {
      "Content-Type": "application/json"
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(task)
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
~~~

## Promise.all

Sometimes you need several independent requests. Running them sequentially can take longer than necessary.

For example:

~~~js
const users = await loadUsers();
const products = await loadProducts();
~~~

If the requests are independent, you can start them together with `Promise.all()`.

~~~js
const [users, products] = await Promise.all([
  loadUsers(),
  loadProducts()
]);
~~~

`Promise.all()` waits until all supplied Promises fulfill. If any of them rejects, the combined Promise rejects.

This is useful for a dashboard that needs several independent pieces of data.

For example, a webshop page might need the current products and the user's cart. A booking dashboard might need rooms and reservations.

## Loading and error states

A real application needs to represent more than successful data.

When loading users, there are normally at least three useful states:

- loading
- success
- error

Conceptually:

~~~js
let loading = true;
let error = null;
let users = [];

try {
  users = await loadUsers();
} catch (err) {
  error = err;
} finally {
  loading = false;
}
~~~

The UI can then respond appropriately.

~~~text
Loading users...
~~~

or:

~~~text
Could not load users. Please try again.
~~~

or the actual user list.

React will give you a much cleaner way to represent these states using component state. The underlying idea remains exactly the same: asynchronous operations can be pending, successful, or failed, and the interface should communicate the current state to the user.

## Why fetch errors need two checks

There are two different kinds of failure to think about.

~~~diagram
{"type": "flow", "title": "Two ways a fetch can fail", "nodes": [{"id": "f", "label": "await fetch(url)", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "net", "label": "Network error", "sub": "promise rejects → catch"}, {"id": "res", "label": "Response arrived", "sub": "even 404 or 500!"}, {"id": "ok", "label": "res.ok?", "highlight": true}, {"id": "json", "label": "await res.json()", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "err", "label": "throw new Error(status)", "kind": "code"}], "edges": [{"from": "f", "to": "net", "dashed": true}, {"from": "f", "to": "res"}, {"from": "res", "to": "ok"}, {"from": "ok", "to": "json", "label": "true"}, {"from": "ok", "to": "err", "label": "false"}]}
~~~

First, the request itself may fail at the network level. For example, the browser might be unable to reach the server. In that situation, `fetch()` rejects.

Second, the server may respond successfully at the HTTP level but return an error status such as 404 or 500. In that situation, `fetch()` normally still resolves with a `Response`, so your code needs to inspect `response.ok`.

That is why this pattern is so common:

~~~js
try {
  const response = await fetch("/api/users");

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const users = await response.json();
  return users;
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
  throw error;
}
~~~

## CORS

**CORS**, or Cross-Origin Resource Sharing, is a browser security mechanism that controls whether a web page from one origin can make certain requests to another origin. An origin is roughly the combination of protocol, host, and port. For example, a React development server running on one port and a Spring Boot server running on another port can be different origins. The backend must provide appropriate CORS response headers to allow the browser to expose the response to the frontend. CORS is enforced by the browser, so a request working in a tool such as a backend client or command-line program does not automatically mean the same request will be allowed from a browser page.

## A practical user-loading flow

Imagine a user management page in your React application.

The flow might look like this:

~~~text
Component starts
      ↓
Set loading = true
      ↓
fetch /api/users
      ↓
Check response.ok
      ↓
Read response.json()
      ↓
Store users
      ↓
Set loading = false
~~~

If the request fails, the error path updates the error state instead.

The important thing is that asynchronous code should have a deliberate success path and failure path. Do not assume that a network request will always succeed.

## Common mistakes

### Forgetting await on response.json()

This is wrong if you need the actual parsed data immediately:

~~~js
const users = response.json();
console.log(users);
~~~

`users` is a Promise here.

Use:

~~~js
const users = await response.json();
~~~

### Assuming fetch rejects for HTTP 404

This is a common misunderstanding.

~~~js
const response = await fetch("/missing-page");
~~~

A 404 response normally does not cause `fetch()` itself to reject. Check `response.ok`.

~~~js
if (!response.ok) {
  throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
}
~~~

### Forgetting to await an async function

Because an async function returns a Promise, this does not give you the final value:

~~~js
const users = loadUsers();
~~~

Instead:

~~~js
const users = await loadUsers();
~~~

or:

~~~js
loadUsers().then(users => {
  console.log(users);
});
~~~

### Running independent requests unnecessarily in sequence

If two requests do not depend on each other, this can be slower:

~~~js
const users = await loadUsers();
const products = await loadProducts();
~~~

Consider:

~~~js
const [users, products] = await Promise.all([
  loadUsers(),
  loadProducts()
]);
~~~

### Ignoring loading and error states

A user should not be left staring at an empty page while a request is running. Likewise, an API failure should not silently produce an empty list that looks like a successful response containing zero records.

Make loading, success, and error states explicit.

## What this means for React

In a React application, asynchronous requests are commonly connected to component state and effects. The component can render a loading message while the request is pending, render an error message when it fails, and render the actual data after success.

The syntax will change slightly when we introduce React, but the underlying JavaScript remains the same:

~~~js
try {
  const response = await fetch("/api/users");

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const users = await response.json();
} catch (error) {
  // Show an error state
}
~~~

You now have the core asynchronous tools needed for frontend-backend communication. Promises represent future results, async/await makes asynchronous code readable, fetch communicates with HTTP APIs, and explicit loading and error handling keeps the interface understandable when the network does not behave perfectly.

